<v Ira Glass>然后，在十二分半钟之后。

00:48:09.870 --> 00:48:13.480
<v Ferdie>Yes, ma'am, thank you very much for holding. I have now Miss Christian on the other line.
<v Ferdie>是的，女士，非常感谢您的等待。我现在让克里斯汀小姐在另一条线上。

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:14.470
<v Christian King>Hi Julie.
<v Christian King>你好，朱莉。

00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:19.310
<v Ferdie>She's from our priority customer support, and Miss Christian will be assisting you further with regards to your concern for today ma'am. OK?
<v Ferdie>她是我们的优先客户支持，克里斯汀小姐将就您今天关心的问题为您提供进一步的协助，女士。好吗？

00:48:19.310 --> 00:48:20.880
<v Julie Snyder>OK, thank you.
<v Julie Snyder>好的，谢谢。

00:48:20.880 --> 00:48:21.910
<v Ferdie>Have a good day, it's up to you Miss Christian.
<v Ferdie>祝您有愉快的一天，克里斯汀小姐，接下来交给您了。

00:48:21.910 --> 00:48:23.580
<v Christian King>OK, thank you so much.
<v Christian King>好的，非常感谢。

00:48:23.580 --> 00:48:29.380
<v Ira Glass>Hi. And I should tell you, we told Ferdie this, we're recording this for possible national radio broadcast on public radio--
<v Ira Glass>你好。我应该告诉你，我们已经告诉弗迪了，我们正在为可能在公共广播电台进行的全国性广播节目进行录音——

00:48:29.380 --> 00:48:32.170
<v Christian King>OK, at this time you don't have our permission to record this, OK?
<v Christian King>好的，此时此刻你们没有我们的许可录音，好吗？

00:48:32.170 --> 00:48:33.000
<v Ira Glass>Why not?
<v Ira Glass>为什么不呢？

00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:43.340
<v Christian King>Because I don't know why you're calling, first of all. Second, you don't have our permission to record this, so I just need to ask that you stop recording the call or else I will have to disconnect and call you back.
<v Christian King>因为首先我不知道你们为什么打电话。其次，你们没有我们的许可录音，所以我只能要求你们停止录音，否则我将不得不挂断电话并回电。

00:48:43.340 --> 00:48:45.960
<v Ira Glass>OK. I will stop recording the call.
<v Ira Glass>好的。我会停止录音。

00:48:45.960 --> 00:49:10.320
<v Ira Glass>Julie tells this woman, Christian King, the history of her case. This takes half an hour. Christian is from the corporate PR office, and she tells us that she can get this solved. She says that somebody's going to call us back tonight or tomorrow, that under no circumstances should we call the regular customer service center. That it was just wrong for us to call up and record like that. We got off the phone, and then we turned back on our tape recorder.
<v Ira Glass>朱莉告诉这个女人，克里斯汀·金，她案件的历史。这花了一个半小时。克里斯汀来自企业公关办公室，她告诉我们她能解决这个问题。她说有人会今晚或明天给我们回电，在任何情况下我们都不应该给普通客户服务中心打电话。我们那样打电话并录音是错误的。我们挂断了电话，然后我们重新打开了录音机。

00:49:10.320 --> 00:49:12.580
<v Ira Glass>So what do you think?
<v Ira Glass>那你觉得怎么样？

00:49:12.580 --> 00:49:44.460
<v Julie Snyder>I mean, I think it's such a crappy system. I think it's such a crappy system that now they're all like, you know, we're on the case and we're going to take care of it and stuff. You know, but don't call here, and say that they're on the air, and don't call and say that you're a journalist and doing a story about that, because that just is rude. But that's the only way that I got through to these people. That's the only way that I got this woman to respond to us. It just makes me so angry.
<v Julie Snyder>我的意思是，我觉得这是一个如此糟糕的系统。我觉得这是一个如此糟糕的系统，以至于现在他们都好像是，你知道，我们正在处理此事，我们会处理好的。你知道，但别打这里，别说你们在直播，别打电话说你们是记者正在做一个关于此的故事，因为那只是粗鲁。但这确实是我能联系到这些人的唯一途径。这也是我能让这个女人回复我们的唯一途径。这真的让我很生气。

00:49:44.460 --> 00:50:22.240
<v Ira Glass>It takes a week and a day, and then a letter arrives by Fedex for Julie, resolving the entire thing. Crediting her account, fixing the mistake. It's done. A few weeks after that, after I do a fair amount of pleading on the phone with MCI, they finally agreed to let us interview somebody in the company. Not Chuck Carter, who I begged for, but instead the senior vice president who runs customer service for MCI. A guy named Jim Myers, who actually started at the bottom handling customer calls himself, and who turns out to be the ideal possible spokesman for MCI. Honestly, sometimes we wondered if they actually just manufactured him in a laboratory somewhere.
<v Ira Glass>过了一周多一天，朱莉收到了一封联邦快递寄来的信，解决了整个问题。贷记了她的账户，修正了错误。搞定了。在那几周后，在我给MCI打了相当多恳求的电话后，他们终于同意让我们采访公司里的某个人。不是我苦苦哀求的查克·卡特，而是负责MCI客户服务的高级副总裁。一个叫吉姆·迈尔斯的家伙，他实际上是从底层处理客户呼叫起家的，结果证明他是MCI理想的发言人。老实说，有时我们怀疑他们是不是真的在某个实验室里制造了他。

00:50:22.240 --> 00:50:24.070
<v Jim Myers>Hi, good morning, it's Jim Myers.
<v Jim Myers>你好，早安，我是吉姆·迈尔斯。

00:50:24.070 --> 00:50:25.210
<v Ira Glass>Hi Jim Myers.
<v Ira Glass>你好，吉姆·迈尔斯。

00:50:25.210 --> 00:50:39.900
<v Jim Myers>Hi Julie, I promise I'm not your enemy. Although after reading the dialogue that has transpired with your account, I'm not-- words can't even describe how you must feel.
<v Jim Myers>你好，朱莉，我保证我不是你的敌人。尽管在阅读了你账户的对话记录后，我不是——言语甚至无法形容你的感受。

00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:41.590
<v Julie Snyder>Oh, that's so nice of you to say.
<v Julie Snyder>噢，你这么说真是太好了。

00:50:41.590 --> 00:50:44.750
<v Jim Myers>And I acknowledge and recognize that we owe you an apology.
<v Jim Myers>而且我承认并意识到我们欠你一个道歉。

00:50:44.750 --> 00:50:47.280
<v Julie Snyder>Oh that's very nice of you to say, thank you.
<v Julie Snyder>噢，你这么说真是太好了，谢谢。

00:50:47.280 --> 00:51:21.260
<v Ira Glass>That's right. He had us at hello. And then he cheerfully answered all of our questions. Yes, it is true, he said. MCI customer care representatives do not have their own individual phone extensions. Yes, it is true, he said, MCI understands that there have been weaknesses in its customer care, and that's why they've been investing millions of dollars in new training. And why MCI has gone on kind of a hiring binge. The number of customer care operators has increased by a third in just one year. It's now 5,300 people at over 15 locations, under Jim's direction.
<v Ira Glass>就是这样。他一开口就征服了我们。然后他愉快地回答了我们所有的问题。是的，是真的，他说。MCI客户关怀代表没有自己的个人电话分机。是的，是真的，他说，MCI理解其客户关怀存在弱点，这就是为什么他们一直在投资数百万美元用于新的培训。这就是为什么MCI一直在进行招聘热潮。客户关怀运营商的数量在一年内增加了三分之一。现在在吉姆的领导下，在15个以上的地点有5300人。

00:51:21.260 --> 00:51:30.810
<v Ira Glass>They've even been testing a system that would solve one of the biggest problems Julie had with MCI. That whenever somebody figured out what was going on with her account, she could never talk to them again.
<v Ira Glass>他们甚至一直在测试一个系统，将解决朱莉在MCI遇到的最大问题之一。即每当有人搞清楚她的账户发生了什么时，她就再也无法与他们交谈。

00:51:30.810 --> 00:51:55.900
<v Jim Myers>It will remember who you spoke to the last time, and it will offer that up to you as a customer or consumer calling in. So what would happen is, Julie, the next time you would call customer service it would say, the last time that you called MCI you spoke to Ira Glass. Ira will be available in about five minutes. If you'd like to speak to Ira, press or say one. Or otherwise, press two and you can talk to the next representative in 30 seconds or whatever the hold time is.
<v Jim Myers>它会记住你上次和谁谈过，并且它会向打进来的客户或消费者提供这个信息。所以会发生的是，朱莉，下次你打电话给客户服务时，它会说，上次你打给MCI时你和艾拉·格拉斯谈过。艾拉大约五分钟后有空。如果你想和艾拉说话，请按或说一。否则，请按二，你可以在30秒或其他等待时间内与下一位代表通话。

00:51:55.900 --> 00:52:22.110
<v Ira Glass>Now one of the things that we need you to respond to is this. Over the course of her calls Julie talked to a service representative named Melanie Gladwell, who told her that she had to contact the complaints division in Iowa. And she said that they don't have a phone number. That she couldn't call the complaints division to straighten out the problem. They only have a fax machine. Do you have a complaint office in Iowa City that doesn't have any telephones, but only a fax machine?
<v Ira Glass>现在我们需要你回应的一点是这个。在她的电话过程中，朱莉与一位叫梅兰妮·格莱德威尔的服务代表谈过，她告诉她必须联系爱荷华州的投诉部门。她说他们没有电话号码。她不能给投诉部门打电话来理顺这个问题。他们只有一个传真机。你们在爱荷华市有一个投诉办公室，没有电话，只有一个传真机吗？

00:52:22.110 --> 00:52:35.010
<v Jim Myers>Well, I wouldn't call it the complaint office. It's the executive escalation office. And they do take phone calls. I'm not sure why that would have been said by Melanie.
<v Jim Myers>嗯，我不会称它为投诉办公室。那是高管升级处理办公室。而且他们确实接听电话。我不确定梅兰妮为什么会那么说。

00:52:35.010 --> 00:52:36.800
<v Julie Snyder>Oh, they do take phone calls.
<v Julie Snyder>噢，他们确实接听电话。

00:52:36.800 --> 00:52:44.670
<v Jim Myers>Yeah. We're a phone company. We should be able to get a phone number for you to just about any department.
<v Jim Myers>是的。我们是电话公司。我们应该能为你提供几乎任何部门的电话号码。

00:52:44.670 --> 00:52:49.090
<v Julie Snyder>That's what I wondered.
<v Julie Snyder>我也是这么想的。

00:52:49.090 --> 00:52:51.610
<v Jim Myers>Was Melanie new by chance?
<v Jim Myers>梅兰妮是碰巧刚入职吗？

00:52:51.610 --> 00:52:53.730
<v Ira Glass>No, Melanie was a supervisor.
<v Ira Glass>不，梅兰妮是一位主管。

00:52:53.730 --> 00:52:57.950
<v Julie Snyder>Yeah, she was priority customer service.
<v Julie Snyder>是的，她是优先客户服务。

00:52:57.950 --> 00:53:04.960
<v Jim Myers>OK. I'll definitely look into that, because it doesn't sound exactly the way things should be.
<v Jim Myers>好的。我一定会调查这件事，因为这听起来并不完全像事情该有的样子。

00:53:04.960 --> 00:53:06.600
<v Julie Snyder>She hated me, though.
<v Julie Snyder>她讨厌我，不过。

00:53:06.600 --> 00:53:07.830
<v Jim Myers>She did not.
<v Jim Myers>她没有。

00:53:07.830 --> 00:53:09.770
<v Julie Snyder>She did.
<v Julie Snyder>她有。

00:53:09.770 --> 00:53:24.530
<v Jim Myers>I can't believe that's possible on anyone here. Now, Julie of all people, my goodness, I can't believe you're even talking with us after this. But we're certainly glad that you are.
<v Jim Myers>我无法相信这对这里的任何人来说是可能的。现在，朱莉，天哪，我无法相信你在经历了这一切后还愿意和我们说话。但我们当然很高兴你愿意。

00:53:24.530 --> 00:53:52.990
<v Julie Snyder>I really appreciate you talking with us today. And I really appreciate the fact that I did get the credit. But I have to say that one of the frustrations is that I feel like, if we hadn't called as the radio show, I think I would still be fighting this. I don't think it would be resolved. I think the only way it got resolved was simply because we're a national radio show, and we're just sort of threatening embarrassment.
<v Julie Snyder>我真的很感激你们今天和我们谈话。而且我真的很感激我确实得到了信用额度。但我必须说，挫折之一是我觉得，如果我们不是以广播节目的名义打的电话，我想我还会为此而战。我不认为这会得到解决。我认为唯一解决它的原因是，仅仅因为我们是一个全国性的广播节目，我们只是在威胁说要让他们难堪。

00:53:52.990 --> 00:54:11.540
<v Jim Myers>Wow. That's actually somewhat disappointing. And I will tell you at some point, I hope you come back and do business with us. And I think you'll see that the tone and the spirit and the delivery of our service is significantly better.
<v Jim Myers>哇。那确实有点令人失望。而且我会告诉你，在某个时刻，我希望你回来和我们做生意。而且我想你会看到我们服务的语气、精神和交付方式有了显著的提高。

00:54:11.540 --> 00:54:12.540
<v Julie Snyder>Thank you.
<v Julie Snyder>谢谢。

00:54:12.540 --> 00:54:32.740
<v Jim Myers>And Julie, I promise, we will treat you like a princess. Maybe I should go out on our VRU and our response and just put my email. And say, look, if your issue's not resolved to your satisfaction, send me an email. Maybe there's an opportunity to go one step more.
<v Jim Myers>还有朱莉，我保证，我们会像对待公主一样对待你。也许我应该去我们的VRU和我们的回复中，把我的电子邮件放上去。并说，看，如果你的问题没有得到你满意的解决，给我发邮件。也许有机会再多做一步。

00:54:32.740 --> 00:54:34.950
<v Julie Snyder>I'm sorry, are you saying that you want us to--
<v Julie Snyder>抱歉，你是说你想让我们——

00:54:34.950 --> 00:54:35.510
<v Jim Myers>Sure--
<v Jim Myers>当然——

00:54:35.510 --> 00:54:37.280
<v Julie Snyder>--broadcast your email address?
<v Julie Snyder>——广播你的电子邮件地址？

00:54:37.280 --> 00:54:38.670
<v Jim Myers>Sure, why not?
<v Jim Myers>当然，为什么不呢？

00:54:38.670 --> 00:54:39.360
<v Julie Snyder>OK.
<v Julie Snyder>好的。

00:54:39.360 --> 00:54:40.570
<v Ira Glass>All right, what's your email address?
<v Ira Glass>好的，你的电子邮件地址是什么？

00:54:40.570 --> 00:54:44.150
<v Jim Myers>[email protected]
<v Jim Myers>[email protected]

00:54:44.150 --> 00:54:50.060
<v Ira Glass>All right, all you MCI customers. You heard it. Let's give that one more time, the email address.
<v Ira Glass>好的，所有MCI的客户。你们听到了。让我们再给一遍，电子邮件地址。

00:54:50.060 --> 00:54:51.460
<v Jim Myers>It's Jim.myers--
<v Jim Myers>是 Jim.myers ——

00:54:51.460 --> 00:54:52.730
<v Ira Glass>M-Y-E-R?
<v Ira Glass>M-Y-E-R?

00:54:52.730 --> 00:54:55.360
<v Jim Myers>M-Y-E-R-S at mci.com.
<v Jim Myers>M-Y-E-R-S at mci.com。

00:54:55.360 --> 00:55:01.760
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:55:01.760 --> 00:55:07.950
<v Jim Myers>Julie, I have your number. I'm going to do something for you. But I'll take it off line.
<v Jim Myers>朱莉，我有你的号码。我要为你做点什么。但我会在线下做。

00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:08.730
<v Julie Snyder>Oh.
<v Julie Snyder>噢。

00:55:08.730 --> 00:55:12.090
<v Ira Glass>No, here we are. Do you want to say?
<v Ira Glass>不，我们在这。你想说吗？

00:55:12.090 --> 00:55:28.200
<v Jim Myers>No, no. I'm going to look through this record. And, you know, I can't pay Julie for her time and whatever. You know. I tend to write people letters and do special things. Just let them-- you know, I'm not going to write on the chalk board a thousand times, I'm sorry for what we did, but I'll do something special for you.
<v Jim Myers>不，不。我要看这个记录。你知道，我不能付钱给朱莉补偿她的时间和什么的。你知道。我倾向于给人们写信做些特别的事情。只要让他们——你知道，我不会在黑板上写一千遍“我对我们所做的感到抱歉”，但我会为你做些特别的事情。

00:55:28.200 --> 00:55:31.130
<v Julie Snyder>Well, I would appreciate anything. Thank you so much.
<v Julie Snyder>好吧，我会感激任何事情。非常感谢。

00:55:31.130 --> 00:55:32.990
<v Jim Myers>We'll do a little something nice for you.
<v Jim Myers>我们会为你做些好事。

00:55:32.990 --> 00:55:33.450
<v Julie Snyder>OK.
<v Julie Snyder>好的。

00:55:33.450 --> 00:55:35.260
<v Jim Myers>OK. All right.
<v Jim Myers>好的。好的。

00:55:35.260 --> 00:55:35.970
<v Julie Snyder>Thanks so much.
<v Julie Snyder>非常感谢。

00:55:35.970 --> 00:55:37.650
<v Jim Myers>All right, bye.
<v Jim Myers>好的，再见。

00:55:37.650 --> 00:55:39.160
<v Julie Snyder>Wow.
<v Julie Snyder>哇。

00:55:39.160 --> 00:55:43.090
<v Ira Glass>Are we being suckers?
<v Ira Glass>我们是被耍了吗？

00:55:43.090 --> 00:55:55.510
<v Julie Snyder>Yes. I think, he said he was going to treat me like a princess. I wonder what I'm getting.
<v Julie Snyder>是的。我想，他说他要像对待公主一样对待我。我想知道我会得到什么。

00:55:55.510 --> 00:55:56.900
<v Ira Glass>Christmas is coming, baby.
<v Ira Glass>圣诞节要到了，宝贝。

00:55:56.900 --> 00:56:13.880
<v Julie Snyder>An ice cream party? You know what, I would love it, is if the gift he sends me is a CD of the music that they play while you're on hold.
<v Julie Snyder>冰淇淋派对？你知道吗，我会很喜欢，如果他送我的礼物是他们在你等待时播放的音乐CD。

00:56:13.880 --> 00:56:52.980
<v Ira Glass>Two days after we talked to Jim Myers, Julie received a huge gift basket from him at her house, filled with chocolate treats and fancy cheeses and gourmet crackers. And a very nice note. And with that, she no longer wanted revenge. For 10 months, the worst thing, the most infuriating thing, was that she was trapped in an impossible situation where there was no court of appeal. As an individual you can't even sue the phone company for all your lost time and your mental anguish. And then, she found the thing all of us always yearn for in this situation. She found a higher judge who could fix it.
<v Ira Glass>在我们和吉姆·迈尔斯交谈两天后，朱莉在她家收到了他送的一大份礼物篮，里面装满了巧克力零食、高级奶酪和美味饼干。还有一张非常好的便条。有了这个，她不再想要报复了。10个月里，最糟糕的事，最令人愤怒的事，是她被困在一个不可能的境地，没有任何上诉法院。作为个人，你甚至不能因为所有失去的时间和精神痛苦起诉电话公司。然后，她找到了我们所有人在这类情况下总是渴望的东西。她找到了一个能解决问题的更高级法官。

00:56:52.980 --> 00:57:32.530
<v Ira Glass>It's now been four years since Julie had her problems and we talked to Jim Myers. And sadly, Jim Myers is no longer at MCI. In fact, MCI no longer exists. They merged with Verizon last year. If you are in a situation where you are having difficulties with your phone company, here's what you can do. You can go to the Better Business Bureau's website, bbb.org, or the FCC's website. They've got a section for this at fcc.gov. Or, for the most effective solution, the one that as we've shown really works, funding for new radio shows is done by the Corporation for Public Broadcasting two times each year. Application dates every April and November.
<v Ira Glass>距离朱莉遇到问题和我们与吉姆·迈尔斯谈话已经过去四年了。遗憾的是，吉姆·迈尔斯不再在MCI工作了。事实上，MCI不再存在了。他们去年与Verizon合并了。如果你遇到与电话公司有困难的情况，你可以这样做。你可以去商业改进局的网站bbb.org，或联邦通信委员会的网站。他们在fcc.gov有一个专门的章节。或者，对于最有效的方案——正如我们展示的，真正有效的——新广播节目的资金是由公共广播公司每年两次资助的。申请日期在每年的4月和11月。

00:57:32.530 --> 00:57:39.700
<v Ira Glass>The program was produced today by Alex Blumberg and myself with Jane Feltes, Diane Cook, Wendy Dorr and Starlee Kine. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder.
<v Ira Glass>该节目由亚历克斯·布鲁姆伯格和我本人制作，简·费尔特斯、黛安·库克、温迪·多尔和斯塔莉·凯恩参与。我们的资深制片人是朱莉·斯奈德。

00:57:39.700 --> 00:57:51.030
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[致谢]

00:57:51.030 --> 00:58:11.740
<v Ira Glass>Our web site, www.thisamericanlife.org. Or as we like to call it, your holiday shopping wonderland. We have signed posters from our last live show tour. We have signed copies of The New Kings of Nonfiction. And a brand new, live CD of a conversation that I had on stage with three of the kings of nonfiction-- writer Susan Orlean, Chuck Klosterman, and Malcolm Gladwell.
